    <md:content-id>m00168</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.1: Determining if Zero Is a Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bf991995-ab3c-4c4f-8a80-decc6af62276</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity </title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is 0 a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">solution of an equation</term> \( 4(x + 2) = 10 \)? Explain.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No. For example: If \( x=0 \), then the expression on the left has the value 8, which makes the equation false.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is 0 a solution for the equation \( 12 - 8x = 3(x + 4) \)? Explain.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. For example: Substituting 0 for \( x \) gives \( 12=12 \), which is true.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is 0 a solution for the equation \( 5x = \frac12 x \)? Explain.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. For example: 5 multiplied by 0 is 0, and \( \frac{1}{2} \) multiplied by 0 is also 0, so the two sides of the equation are equal.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is 0 a solution for the equation \( \frac {6}{x} + 1 = 8 \)? Explain.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No. For example: Dividing by 0 gives an undefined result, so it cannot be added by 1 to equal 8.</para>
  </solution></exercise></section>
